questions.question:
The time period of a simple harmonic oscillator is
T = 2\pi \sqrt{\frac{m}{k}}
The measured value of mass (m) of the object is 10 g with an accuracy of 10 mg, and time for 50 oscillations of the spring is found to be 60 s using a watch of 2 s resolution. Percentage error in determination of spring constant(k) is ______ %.

questions.explanation:
The time period of a simple harmonic oscillator is given by \(T = 2\pi \sqrt{\frac{m}{k}}\).
Squaring both sides, we get \(T^2 = 4\pi^2 \frac{m}{k}\).
Rearranging for the spring constant \(k\):
k = 4\pi^2 \frac{m}{T^2}
To find the percentage error in \(k\), we use the formula for propagation of errors:
\frac{\Delta k}{k} = \frac{\Delta m}{m} + 2\frac{\Delta T}{T}
Given data:
• Mass \(m = 10\) g
• Accuracy in mass \(\Delta m = 10\) mg \(= 10 \times 10^{-3}\) g
• Total time for 50 oscillations \(t = 60\) s
• Resolution of watch \(\Delta t = 2\) s
• Number of oscillations \(N = 50\)
First, calculate the time period \(T\) and its uncertainty \(\Delta T\):
T = \frac{\text{Total time}}{\text{Number of oscillations}} = \frac{60\text{ s}}{50} = 1.2\text{ s}
\Delta T = \frac{\text{Resolution of watch}}{\text{Number of oscillations}} = \frac{2\text{ s}}{50} = 0.04\text{ s}
Now, substitute these values into the error propagation formula:
\frac{\Delta k}{k} = \frac{10 \times 10^{-3}}{10} + 2 \times \frac{0.04}{1.2}
\frac{\Delta k}{k} = 0.001 + 2 \times \frac{4}{120}
\frac{\Delta k}{k} = 0.001 + 2 \times \frac{1}{30}
\frac{\Delta k}{k} = 0.001 + 0.06666...
\frac{\Delta k}{k} \approx 0.06766
Percentage error in \(k\) is:
\% \text{ Error} = \frac{\Delta k}{k} \times 100\% = 0.06766 \times 100\% \approx 6.76\%

Given data for the cylindrical wire:
m = (0.4 \pm 0.01) \text{ g}
\ell = (8 \pm 0.04) \text{ cm}
r = (6 \pm 0.03) \text{ mm}
The density \(\rho\) of a cylindrical wire is given by the formula:
\rho = \frac{m}{\pi r^2 \ell}
To find the maximum percentage error in density, we use the method of logarithmic differentiation. Taking the natural logarithm on both sides:
\ln \rho = \ln m - \ln \pi - 2 \ln r - \ln \ell
Differentiating both sides with respect to the respective variables:
\frac{d\rho}{\rho} = \frac{dm}{m} - 0 - 2\frac{dr}{r} - \frac{d\ell}{\ell}
For maximum fractional error, the absolute values of individual fractional errors are added:
\left(\frac{\Delta \rho}{\rho}\right)_{\text{max}} = \left|\frac{\Delta m}{m}\right| + \left|-2\frac{\Delta r}{r}\right| + \left|-\frac{\Delta \ell}{\ell}\right|
\left(\frac{\Delta \rho}{\rho}\right)_{\text{max}} = \frac{\Delta m}{m} + 2\frac{\Delta r}{r} + \frac{\Delta \ell}{\ell}
Substitute the given values:
\left(\frac{\Delta \rho}{\rho}\right)_{\text{max}} = \frac{0.01}{0.4} + 2\left(\frac{0.03}{6}\right) + \frac{0.04}{8}
Calculate each term:
\frac{0.01}{0.4} = 0.025
2\left(\frac{0.03}{6}\right) = 2(0.005) = 0.01
\frac{0.04}{8} = 0.005
Summing these values to find the maximum fractional error:
\left(\frac{\Delta \rho}{\rho}\right)_{\text{max}} = 0.025 + 0.01 + 0.005 = 0.04
The maximum percentage error in density is:
\text{Percentage error} = \left(\frac{\Delta \rho}{\rho}\right)_{\text{max}} \times 100\% = 0.04 \times 100\% = 4\%

